<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahdist War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahdist War was fought from 1881 to 1899 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> between the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a>, who proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, and the Egyptian government backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The Mahdists took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> in 1885, killing Charles Gordon, and built their own state, which an Anglo-Egyptian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Herbert Kitchener</a> destroyed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahdist-war.jpg" alt="Mahdist War" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fidodog14 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahdist_War_Collage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After Muhammad Ali's invasion of 1820 to 1821, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> was governed as part of the Ottoman-Egyptian state, known locally as the Turkiyah. Large sections of the population suffered heavy taxation, forced labour and coercion, and many people left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> remained a major part of the economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s debts under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Isma'il Pasha</a> led to his forced abdication in 1879. In 1873 Isma'il had appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a> governor of the Equatorial Provinces, and Gordon tried to suppress the slave trade, but resigned in 1880. Resentment of Turco-Egyptian rule grew among merchants, tribal leaders and religious figures, and the British government refused to assume direct responsibility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the 1870s the cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a> preached renewal of the faith and proclaimed himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, the promised redeemer. In August 1881 two Egyptian companies sent to arrest him fired on each other in the dark and were destroyed in turn at the Battle of Aba. He retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> and declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, calling his followers the Ansar. On 7 June 1882 his army surprised an Egyptian force of 4,000 under Yusef Pasha and destroyed it. In November 1883 the Mahdi, with about 40,000 men, defeated the army of the retired colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-hicks">William Hicks</a> at the Battle of Shaykan, and only about 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> survived.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> then decided that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> should evacuate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and sent Gordon, who reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> on 18 February 1884. He grew reluctant to leave while any who wished to go had not been able to, and it is debated whether he deliberately stayed too long. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines were cut on 15 March. A relief column of 9,000 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> won at Abu Klea and Abu Kru in January 1885, but Khartoum fell on 26 January 1885, after a siege of 313 days, and Gordon and the garrison were killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad">Muhammad Ahmad</a> died on 22 June 1885 and was succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallahi-ibn-muhammad">Khalifa Abdallahi</a> ibn Muhammad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Mahdist state fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in the 1880s and Italian troops in the 1890s. In 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Herbert Kitchener</a> led an Anglo-Egyptian force of 8,200 British soldiers and 17,600 Egyptian and Sudanese soldiers up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> with railways and gunboats. After the Battle of Atbara in April the force reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a>, the Mahdist capital, on 2 September, where the Mahdist army of more than 60,000 attacked and was cut down by machine guns and rifles. Organised resistance ended the next year, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-condominium">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a>, a condominium in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> held de facto control, was created in 1899.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahdist War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
